    "knowledge_point": "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function",
    "problem": "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.",
    "worked_solution": "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |",
